@page "/layout"

<PageTitle>Layout animations - Bit.Bmotion</PageTitle>

<h1>Layout animations</h1>

<p class="page-intro">
    Layout properties are the ones you cannot animate cheaply: change <code>justify-content</code>
    or a width and the browser reflows, it does not tween. FLIP sidesteps that - measure where the
    element was (<strong>F</strong>irst), let the real layout change happen (<strong>L</strong>ast),
    <strong>I</strong>nvert the difference with a transform, then <strong>P</strong>lay it back to
    zero. The result animates on the compositor while the DOM is already in its final state.
    <code>Layout="true"</code> is the whole opt-in; Bmotion does the measuring.
</p>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Animating a reflow</h2>
    <p>
        Add <strong>Layout="true"</strong> to any Bmotion element and every re-render that moves or
        resizes it plays a FLIP. Nothing below animates a property directly: the buttons change the
        list order and the container's <code>justify-content</code>, and the boxes work out the rest
        for themselves.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card layout-stage">
            <div class="layout-strip" style="@($"justify-content:{_justifyContent}")">
                @foreach (var item in _items)
                {
                    <Bmotion @key="@item.Label"
                            Layout="true"
                            Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 500, damping: 35)">
                        <div class="@($"layout-tile tone-{item.Tone}")">@item.Label</div>
                    </Bmotion>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="Shuffle">Shuffle</button>
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="ToggleLayout">Justify: @_justifyContent</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_layoutCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Shared elements</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>LayoutId</strong> connects elements <em>across</em> mounts. When one unmounts and
        another mounts carrying the same id, the new one FLIPs from where the old one was - so a
        single underline appears to slide between tabs even though each tab renders its own.
        <code>Layout="BmLayout.Position"</code> animates position without scaling, which is what
        you want on anything containing text. Wrap independent groups in
        <code>&lt;BmotionLayoutGroup Name="…"&gt;</code> to keep their ids from colliding.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card tab-stage">
            <div class="tab-strip" role="tablist">
                @foreach (var tab in _tabs)
                {
                    var t = tab;
                    <div class="tab @(t == _activeTab ? "active" : null)"
                         role="tab" tabindex="0" aria-selected="@(t == _activeTab)"
                         @onclick="@(() => _activeTab = t)"
                         @onkeydown="@(e => { if (e.Key is "Enter" or " ") _activeTab = t; })">
                        @t
                        @if (t == _activeTab)
                        {
                            <Bmotion LayoutId="tab-underline"
                                    Layout="BmLayout.Position"
                                    Transition="Bm.Tween(0.3, BmEase.InOut)">
                                <div class="tab-underline" />
                            </Bmotion>
                        }
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_layoutIdCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Size correction</h2>
    <p>
        Animating a box's size by scaling it is fast but wrong: the text inside stretches and the
        corners go oval. With <strong>Layout="true"</strong> Bmotion still scales - that is what
        keeps it on the compositor - but <strong>counter-scales the children</strong> and
        <strong>corrects the border-radius</strong> every frame, so the label and the inner dot stay
        the size and shape they were. Grow the card and watch what does <em>not</em> distort.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card resize-stage">
            <Bmotion Layout="true" Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 350, damping: 30)">
                <div class="@(_big ? "resize-card big" : "resize-card")">
                    <span class="resize-label">Layout</span>
                    <div class="resize-dot" />
                </div>
            </Bmotion>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="@(() => _big = !_big)">@(_big ? "Shrink" : "Grow")</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_sizeCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Anchoring and measurement</h2>
    <p>
        A FLIP is a comparison between two measurements, and four parameters decide what is being
        compared. <strong>LayoutAnchor</strong> picks the point that appears to stay still: the
        default top-left corner is right for text and rows, while
        <code>BmLayoutAnchor.Center</code> makes a box grow outward evenly - the same size change,
        read completely differently.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack resize-stage">
            <span class="demo-label">anchor: TopLeft (default)</span>
            <Bmotion Layout="true"
                     LayoutDependency="_anchored"
                     OnLayoutAnimationComplete="@(() => { _layoutCompletions++; StateHasChanged(); })"
                     Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 350, damping: 30)">
                <div class="@(_anchored ? "anchor-box big" : "anchor-box")" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>

        <div class="demo-card stack resize-stage">
            <span class="demo-label">anchor: Center</span>
            <Bmotion Layout="true"
                     LayoutAnchor="BmLayoutAnchor.Center"
                     LayoutDependency="_anchored"
                     Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 350, damping: 30)">
                <div class="@(_anchored ? "anchor-box big" : "anchor-box")" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="@(() => _anchored = !_anchored)">@(_anchored ? "Shrink" : "Grow")</button>
        <span class="demo-label">layout animations completed: @_layoutCompletions</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        The other three are about the coordinate space. <strong>LayoutScroll</strong> is for an
        element inside a scrolling container: without it, scrolling that container between the two
        measurements looks exactly like the element having moved, and it jumps.
        <strong>LayoutRoot</strong> does the same job for a <code>position: fixed</code> element,
        which stays put while the page scrolls under it. <strong>LayoutDependency</strong> is the
        performance one - measuring forces a reflow, so pointing it at the state the layout actually
        depends on stops unrelated re-renders paying for one.
    </p>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_projectionCode" />
</div>

<style>
    /* Fixed footprint so the two anchor stages are directly comparable: the same size change
       reads differently only because of where it is projected from. */
    .anchor-box {
        width: 3.5rem;
        height: 3.5rem;
        border-radius: 10px;
        background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bm-primary), var(--bm-secondary));
    }

    .anchor-box.big { width: 7rem; height: 7rem; }
</style>

<style>
    .layout-stage { align-items: stretch; }

    .layout-strip { display: flex; gap: .75rem; flex-wrap: wrap; width: 100%; }

    /* 20px at 700 is deliberate rather than decorative: at that size and weight the label counts
       as large text, which is the bar the lighter tones below can actually clear against white. */
    .layout-tile {
        width: 3.75rem;
        height: 3.75rem;
        display: flex;
        color: #fff;
        font-weight: 700;
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        border-radius: 8px;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
    }

    /* Five distinguishable tiles, fixed rather than derived from the theme's accents.

       They were derived once, and it did not survive the dark theme: the accent that is dark
       enough for a white label in the light theme is the light end of the ramp in the dark one,
       where the same label stops reading. What these tiles are demonstrating is FLIP, not colour,
       so they are pinned to five shades that each clear 4.5:1 against white in both themes and
       stay clearly apart from one another. */
    .tone-0 { background: #1276C6; }
    .tone-1 { background: #124265; }
    .tone-2 { background: #C25520; }
    .tone-3 { background: #7E3714; }
    .tone-4 { background: #4D4D4D; }

    .tab-stage { align-items: flex-start; }

    .tab-strip { display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; }

    /* position: relative is what the shared underline FLIPs against - it is absolutely placed
       inside whichever tab is active. */
    .tab {
        cursor: pointer;
        font-weight: 600;
        position: relative;
        padding-bottom: 6px;
        color: var(--bm-muted);
    }

    .tab.active { color: var(--bm-text); }

    .tab:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--bm-primary); outline-offset: 4px; }

    .tab-underline {
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        bottom: 0;
        height: 3px;
        position: absolute;
        border-radius: 2px;
        background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--bm-primary), var(--bm-secondary));
    }

    .resize-stage { min-height: 13.75rem; }

    .resize-card {
        gap: .6rem;
        width: 7.5rem;
        height: 7.5rem;
        display: flex;
        border-radius: 20px;
        align-items: center;
        flex-direction: column;
        justify-content: center;
        background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bm-primary), var(--bm-secondary));
    }

    .resize-card.big { width: 16.25rem; height: 11.25rem; }

    .resize-label { font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.3rem; color: #fff; }

    .resize-dot { width: 2.125rem; height: 2.125rem; border-radius: 50%; background: rgba(255, 255, 255, .85); }
</style>

@code {
    private readonly string[] _tabs = ["Home", "Projects", "About", "Contact"];
    private string _activeTab = "Home";
    private bool _big;
    private bool _anchored;
    private int _layoutCompletions;
    private string _justifyContent = "flex-start";

    /// <summary>A shuffled tile: its label and which of the palette's five tones it wears.</summary>
    private record ColoredItem(string Label, int Tone);

    private List<ColoredItem> _items = [new("A", 0), new("B", 1), new("C", 2), new("D", 3), new("E", 4)];

    void Shuffle() => _items = [.. _items.OrderBy(_ => Random.Shared.Next())];

    void ToggleLayout() => _justifyContent = _justifyContent switch
    {
        "flex-start" => "center",
        "center"     => "flex-end",
        _            => "flex-start"
    };

    private const string _layoutCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        <style>
            :root { --bm-primary: #1276C6; --bm-primary-text: #fff; --bm-card: #F5F5F5; --bm-border: #E0E0E0; }
            .strip { display: flex; gap: .75rem; flex-wrap: wrap; width: 100%; }
            .tile {
                width: 60px; height: 60px; border-radius: 8px; color: #fff;
                display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-weight: 700;
            }
            .btn-bm {
                background: var(--bm-primary); color: var(--bm-primary-text); border: none; border-radius: 8px;
                padding: .6rem 1.4rem; font-weight: 600; cursor: pointer;
            }
        </style>

        @* Nothing here animates a property. The buttons change the ORDER and the container's
           justify-content; each tile measures the difference and FLIPs into place. @key is
           what lets Blazor move a tile instead of rewriting it. *@
        <div class="strip" style="@($"justify-content:{_justifyContent}")">
            @foreach (var item in _items)
            {
                <Bmotion @key="@item.Label" Layout="true"
                         Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 500, damping: 35)">
                    <div class="tile" style="@($"background:{item.Color}")">@item.Label</div>
                </Bmotion>
            }
        </div>

        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="Shuffle">Shuffle</button>
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="ToggleLayout">Justify: @_justifyContent</button>

        @code {
            private string _justifyContent = "flex-start";

            private record ColoredItem(string Label, string Color);

            private List<ColoredItem> _items = [
                new("A", "#1276C6"), new("B", "#4A83B8"), new("C", "#FD7F36"),
                new("D", "#0E5895"), new("E", "#B25926"),
            ];

            void Shuffle() => _items = [.. _items.OrderBy(_ => Random.Shared.Next())];

            void ToggleLayout() => _justifyContent = _justifyContent switch
            {
                "flex-start" => "center",
                "center"     => "flex-end",
                _            => "flex-start"
            };
        }
        """;

    private const string _layoutIdCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* Each tab renders its OWN underline - but they share a LayoutId, so the one that
           mounts FLIPs from where the one that unmounted was. The illusion is a single
           underline sliding across. *@
        @foreach (var tab in _tabs)
        {
            var t = tab;
            <div class="tab" style="position:relative" @onclick="@(() => _activeTab = t)">
                @t
                @if (t == _activeTab)
                {
                    @* Position only: scaling an underline between tabs of different widths
                       would squash it. *@
                    <Bmotion LayoutId="tab-underline"
                             Layout="BmLayout.Position"
                             Transition="Bm.Tween(0.3, BmEase.InOut)">
                        <div style="position:absolute;bottom:0;left:0;right:0;height:3px;background:#1276C6" />
                    </Bmotion>
                }
            </div>
        }

        @* Reuse the same id in independent groups by namespacing it: *@
        <BmotionLayoutGroup Name="sidebar">
            ... <Bmotion LayoutId="tab-underline"><div /></Bmotion> ...
        </BmotionLayoutGroup>
        """;

    private const string _sizeCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        <style>
            :root { --bm-primary: #1276C6; --bm-secondary: #FD7F36; }
            .resize-card {
                width: 120px; height: 120px; border-radius: 20px; gap: .6rem;
                display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
                background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bm-primary), var(--bm-secondary));
            }
            .resize-card.big { width: 260px; height: 180px; }
            .resize-label { font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.3rem; color: #fff; }
            .resize-dot { width: 34px; height: 34px; border-radius: 50%; background: rgba(255,255,255,.85); }
        </style>

        @* The box scales - that is what keeps the animation on the compositor - but the
           children are counter-scaled and the border-radius is corrected each frame, so the
           label and the dot never distort and the corners stay round. *@
        <Bmotion Layout="true" Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 350, damping: 30)">
            <div class="@(_big ? "resize-card big" : "resize-card")">
                <span class="resize-label">Layout</span>
                <div class="resize-dot" />
            </div>
        </Bmotion>

        @code {
            private bool _big;
        }
        """;

    private const string _projectionCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* LayoutAnchor picks the point that appears to stay still while the box resizes.
           TopLeft (the default) pins the corner; Center grows the box outward evenly. *@
        <Bmotion Layout="true" LayoutAnchor="BmLayoutAnchor.Center">
            <div class="box" />
        </Bmotion>

        @* LayoutScroll: the element lives in a scrolling container, so its measurements fold in
           that container's scroll offset. Without it, scrolling between the two measurements
           reads as the element having moved and it visibly jumps. *@
        <div class="scroller" style="overflow:auto; max-height:20rem">
            <Bmotion Layout="true" LayoutScroll="true">
                <div class="row" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>

        @* LayoutRoot: same idea for a position:fixed element, which stays put while the page
           scrolls under it - so it is measured against the viewport, not the document. *@
        <Bmotion Layout="true" LayoutRoot="true">
            <div class="toolbar" style="position:fixed; top:0" />
        </Bmotion>

        @* LayoutDependency: measuring forces a reflow. Point it at the state the layout
           actually depends on and unrelated re-renders stop paying for one.
           OnLayoutAnimationStart / OnLayoutAnimationComplete bracket the animation. *@
        <Bmotion Layout="true"
                 LayoutDependency="_isOpen"
                 OnLayoutAnimationStart="@(() => _settling = true)"
                 OnLayoutAnimationComplete="@(() => _settling = false)">
            <div class="panel" />
        </Bmotion>

        @code {
            private bool _isOpen;
            private bool _settling;
        }
        """;
}
